    "id": "idea-1",
    "kind": "discovery",
    "title": "Why do Collatz-like maps with small multipliers rarely diverge?",
    "question": "For generalized Collatz maps n→n/2 (even) and n→kn+1 (odd), how does the heuristic expected log-drift, ln(k)/2 versus ln(2)/2, predict which k yield bounded trajectories, and where does empirical computation deviate?",
    "why_it_attracts_me": "A simple probabilistic argument predicts the fate of an unsolved-looking family of maps. I want to see how cleanly the heuristic matches brute-force data for k=1,3,5,7, and where cycles or outliers break the story.",
    "first_work_unit": "Derive the expected log-drift per step for odd multiplier k, then compute trajectories for all starting n below 100,000 for k=1,3,5, recording max values, stopping times, and any non-terminating or cycling cases.",
    "deliverable": "A short report with the derived drift formula, a table comparing predicted and observed behavior for each k, and notes on any cycles found.",
    "brief_checklist": [
      "Drift formula derived and stated explicitly",
      "Empirical results tabulated for at least three values of k",
      "Discrepancies or cycles between heuristic and data explicitly discussed"
    ]
